course: dictionary
lesson: 8109
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Rent» is one of these words. It can be a noun, meaning money you pay regularly, or a verb, meaning to pay to use something for a time. «Fixed costs are bills that come every month, like rent, energy and council tax» shows the noun: rent is money paid every month.
explain: The verb is about using something that belongs to someone else. «Britain rented the New Territories in 1898 for 99 years» means Britain paid to use that land for a long time. When you meet rent, ask: is someone paying money to live somewhere, or to use something for a while?
explain: Look for clues around the word. «In a rented home the landlord is supposed to fix it» tells you the home belongs to the landlord, and the renter pays to live there. The word «rent out» means the owner lets someone else use their property in return for money.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in. If «pay to use» fits the sentence and makes sense, you have found the right meaning. If it does not fit, try another meaning from the entry.
fact: Rent is money paid regularly to use a home, flat, room or land that belongs to someone else.
fact: As a verb, «rent» means to pay to use something that belongs to someone else for a time, like «rent a bike».
fact: «Rent out» means to let someone use your property in return for money.
fact: «Rent-free» means you do not have to pay any rent.
fact: An old meaning of «rent» is a tear or split in cloth. It comes from the past of «rend», which means to tear.
exercise: «Fixed costs are bills that come every month, like rent, energy and council tax.» What does rent mean here? | *Money paid every month to live in a home | A tear in a piece of cloth | To split something in two | A kind of bike
exercise: «Britain rented the New Territories in 1898 for 99 years.» What does rented mean here? | Built a new town on the land | Gave the land away for ever | Tore the land apart | *Paid to use land that belonged to someone else for a time
exercise: «Sam will ___ his spare room to a student for money.» Which word fits the gap? | tear | rend | rent-free | *rent out
test: «Rent-free» means what? | You must pay twice the rent | You have torn a piece of cloth | *You do not have to pay any rent | You are cleaning a house for money
test: «In a rented home the landlord is supposed to fix the damp.» Who owns the home? | The damp | The children who live there | *The landlord | The tenant, who pays the rent
exam: Which sentence uses rent as a verb? | It was rent-free for the whole year | The rent is due on Monday | The old shirt had a rent in the sleeve | *They rent a flat by the sea
